			<div class="content" id="post-content-2842020">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Details about the exact nature of this object were withheld by the older Agent Graeme.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think, for this to work, the Foundation isn't allowed to know exactly what it is. But don't worry, I've figured it out. Here's what it is and how they get ahold of it:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;potential&nbsp;spoiler</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;potential&nbsp;spoiler</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It's literally just a time machine. There's a related detail that I think is similarly important:</p>
<p>In case you didn't catch it, Agent Graeme doesn't age.</p>
<p>Here's how it happened:</p>
<p>0. The 'single-use' time machine is not really single-use. Agent Graeme just hid it from the Foundation.</p>
<p>1. In each iteration of the loop, he spends some time fixing it. (Gap between ten years, four months ago and ten years, one month ago in the timeline)</p>
<p>2. He stole one of the EPTF generators from the containment area. (Six years, one week ago)</p>
<p>3. He travelled back in time to before his own birth with the time machine. (Six years ago; this is why he disappears)</p>
<p>4. Using the EPTF generator, he manipulated the timeframe of his own birth, making it slightly later with each iteration of the loop.</p>
<p>5. Finally, he travelled forward in time to relinquish the time machine to the Foundation, at exactly the time they needed it. (Five months from now)</p>
<p>I do slightly regret just making it basically a rule-free time machine, but it is basically linked to the loop of this anomaly; the Foundation can't just use it willy-nilly. And yes, its very existence is another bootstrap paradox.</p>
</div>
</div>
